# Why These Views Are Recommendations

The database schema included in this repository represents the **core operational data model** of the Talent Prism platform.

Views, however, are part of the **application layer**. They are shaped by the needs of dashboards, APIs, reporting systems, and business workflows. As a result, there is no single "correct" set of views that fits every implementation.

The examples in this document are intended to demonstrate common architectural patterns used in production systems. They provide a starting point that developers can adapt, extend, or replace based on the specific requirements of their own applications.

In general, a good database view should:

- Present only the information required for a specific use case.
- Hide unnecessary implementation details from the application.
- Reduce the need for repetitive SQL joins.
- Simplify API development and reporting.
- Improve readability and maintainability without duplicating data.

As your application evolves, you may create additional views for analytics, exports, dashboards, machine learning pipelines, or any other workflow that benefits from a simplified, read-only representation of your operational data.